<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madhvacharya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1238–1317</span> · Also known as Madhva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madhvacharya was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> philosopher from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvaita-vedanta">Dvaita</a>, or dualist, school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>. Against Advaita, he taught that God, identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, is eternally distinct from souls and matter. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> temple at Udupi and its monasteries, which still lead his tradition. The dates 1238–1317 are traditional.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madhvacharya.jpg" alt="Madhvacharya" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AR767 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shri_Madhvacharya_Vigraha,_Pajaka,_Udupi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
